  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, equation (1.1) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0; q : R × R → R^m; A is a constant m × m real matrix",
      "observation": "The inherited setting is a homogeneous first-order system in one spatial variable x and time t, with m real-valued unknown components. Subscripts denote partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "hyperbolic if the matrix A has real eigenvalues and a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors",
      "observation": "The stated constant-coefficient hyperbolicity criterion requires both real eigenvalues and a complete independent set of corresponding eigenvectors."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening paragraph immediately following the hyperbolicity criterion",
      "anchor": "any vector in R^m can be uniquely decomposed as a linear combination of these eigenvectors",
      "observation": "The eigenvectors provide a basis for the real state space. The paragraph also identifies the corresponding eigenvalues as wave speeds."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, Section 1.1, equations (1.8)–(1.9)",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + f(q(x,t))_x = 0; where f(q) is the flux function; q_t + f′(q)q_x = 0",
      "observation": "The conservation law is rewritten in quasilinear form, replacing the constant coefficient matrix by the flux Jacobian evaluated at the state."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, sentence immediately after equation (1.9)",
      "anchor": "the equation is hyperbolic if the flux Jacobian matrix f′(q) satisfies the conditions previously given for the matrix A",
      "observation": "The selected claim transfers the real-eigenvalue and complete-independent-eigenvector criterion to the flux Jacobian."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, continuation of Section 1.1",
      "anchor": "linear flux function f(q) = Aq; f(q) is a nonlinear function of q, a vector of conserved quantities",
      "observation": "The flux may be linear or nonlinear, and q has the role of a vector of conserved quantities."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, Section 1.1.2, opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "provided that q and f(q) are sufficiently smooth",
      "observation": "The source qualifies passage from the integral conservation law to its differential form by smoothness and notes that interesting solutions may contain discontinuities."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "Inherited setting: x and t are real spatial and temporal variables, and q : R × R → R^m is the vector-valued unknown; m counts its components [raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, equation (1.1) and following paragraph].",
      "The flux f acts on the state q, and f′(q) is its m × m flux Jacobian; a flux between real m-dimensional state spaces is inherited from the system's dimensions, although no state-domain set is specified [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, equations (1.8)–(1.9); raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, definition of q and A].",
      "At a state where the criterion is applied, there must exist m corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors with real eigenvalues. Indexing these pairs by i = 1, …, m is analyst notation for the source's 'set of m' [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening hyperbolicity criterion and sentence after equation (1.9)]."
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The equation under discussion is the one-dimensional homogeneous conservation law q_t + (f(q))_x = 0, written in quasilinear form q_t + f′(q)q_x = 0 [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, equations (1.8)–(1.9)].",
      "The flux Jacobian f′(q) has real eigenvalues and m linearly independent corresponding eigenvectors, with the real-space interpretation supported by the stated decomposition of every vector in R^m [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening paragraph and sentence after equation (1.9)].",
      "Existence of the derivatives and applicability of the chain rule are implicit in the displayed quasilinear rewriting; no precise differentiability class is stated there [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, 'Rewriting this in the quasilinear form' and equation (1.9)]."
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "The conservation-law equation is hyperbolic if its flux Jacobian satisfies the preceding real-eigenvalue and complete-independent-eigenvector criterion for A [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, sentence immediately after equation (1.9)]."
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "Subscripts t and x denote partial derivatives with respect to time and space [raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, paragraph following equation (1.1)].",
      "f(q) is the flux function, while f′(q) denotes its Jacobian matrix with respect to the vector state q [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, text surrounding equations (1.8)–(1.9)].",
      "The referenced hyperbolicity condition includes a full eigenvector basis, not merely real eigenvalues: the source explicitly requires m independent eigenvectors and states that every vector in R^m has a unique expansion in them [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening paragraph].",
      "The constant linear system fits the conservation-law form through f(q) = Aq [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, final sentence continued on raw PDF page 26, printed page 4]."
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The selected passage concerns first-order systems in one spatial dimension; the source explicitly restricts the book's discussion to first-order hyperbolic systems [raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, introduction to equation (1.1); raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, paragraph following equation (1.7)].",
      "The pointwise spectral criterion is inherited from a constant real matrix, but f′(q) may vary with q because nonlinear fluxes are allowed [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, equation (1.9); raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, opening paragraph].",
      "The differential and quasilinear expressions belong to a smooth setting. The source separately warns that conservation-law solutions can be discontinuous and qualifies derivation of the differential form from the integral form by sufficient smoothness [raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, Section 1.1.2, opening paragraph]."
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Spectral completeness and scalar field",
        "details": [
          "Real eigenvalues and m independent corresponding eigenvectors are jointly required; reality of the eigenvalues alone is insufficient to express the stated criterion [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening paragraph].",
          "The source's unique decomposition of vectors in R^m identifies a real eigenvector basis as the intended meaning [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, sentence immediately following the criterion]."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Repeated eigenvalues",
        "details": [
          "The criterion imposes independence of eigenvectors without imposing pairwise distinct eigenvalues. Repeated eigenvalues are therefore not excluded when a full eigenvector basis exists; this is an inference from the exact listed conditions [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening hyperbolicity criterion]."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "State dependence",
        "details": [
          "The matrix tested is f′(q), evaluated at the state q, rather than the flux value f(q) [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, equation (1.9) and following sentence].",
          "No state-independent choice of eigenvectors or eigenvalues, nor any regularity of such choices across states, is stated [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, sentence after equation (1.9)]."
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Nature of the claim",
        "details": [
          "The selected sentence supplies a hyperbolicity criterion. It does not state an existence, uniqueness, or regularity theorem for solutions [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, sentence after equation (1.9)].",
          "The wording 'suggests' connects the quasilinear rewriting to the criterion; the preceding constant-coefficient paragraph presents the referenced criterion as the meaning of hyperbolicity [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening paragraph and text after equation (1.9)]."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Apply the spectral condition to the flux Jacobian f′(q), not to f(q) itself [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, equation (1.9) and following sentence].",
    "Preserve both real eigenvalues and m linearly independent corresponding eigenvectors [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening paragraph, explicitly referenced after equation (1.9)].",
    "Preserve the real m-dimensional state-space interpretation of the eigenvector basis [raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, definition of q; raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, unique decomposition in R^m].",
    "Do not strengthen the stated criterion to require distinct eigenvalues: distinctness is absent from the referenced list of conditions [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening hyperbolicity criterion].",
    "Retain the conservation-law and quasilinear-system context q_t + (f(q))_x = 0 and q_t + f′(q)q_x = 0 [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, equations (1.8)–(1.9)].",
    "Do not assert that the quasilinear rewriting holds classically for arbitrary discontinuous solutions; the source explicitly recognizes discontinuities and smoothness qualifications [raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, Section 1.1.2, opening paragraph]."
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "The state domain and global quantifier for hyperbolicity are not specified.",
      "evidence": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, sentence after equation (1.9), states the condition on f′(q) without naming an admissible state set or explicitly saying 'for every q'.",
      "impact": "The spectral criterion can be recorded at a state. Extending it to all states in a chosen domain requires making the domain and quantifier explicit as an interpretation."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The exact regularity assumptions needed for the quasilinear rewriting are unstated.",
      "evidence": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, equations (1.8)–(1.9), gives the rewriting without a differentiability class; raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, Section 1.1.2, uses 'sufficiently smooth' for deriving differential form from integral form.",
      "impact": "Existence of a Jacobian and validity of the chain rule are implicit, but specific global C¹ or stronger assumptions cannot be attributed to the selected sentence."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The selected sentence gives an 'if' criterion with suggestive wording rather than a displayed biconditional.",
      "evidence": "Raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, text following equation (1.9), says the rewriting 'suggests that the equation is hyperbolic if'; the opening paragraph introduces the constant-coefficient condition as a definition.",
      "impact": "A definitional reading is supported by the cross-reference, but the selected sentence does not separately state or prove a converse."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The range of the component count m is not formally quantified.",
      "evidence": "Raw PDF page 23, printed page 1, describes m components and explicitly discusses the scalar case m = 1, without a formal bound on m.",
      "impact": "The source uses an ordinary finite system-size parameter; it does not explicitly address a zero-dimensional exceptional case."
    }
  ],
  "contract_plain_english": "For the one-dimensional conservation law q_t + (f(q))_x = 0, the quasilinear form is q_t + f′(q)q_x = 0. The source says the equation is hyperbolic if the flux Jacobian satisfies the earlier condition for a constant real coefficient matrix: real eigenvalues together with m corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors forming a basis of R^m [raw PDF page 25, printed page 3, opening paragraph and equations (1.8)–(1.9) with the following sentence; real-state setting on raw PDF page 23, printed page 1]. The passage does not require distinct eigenvalues, specify the state domain over which the condition must hold, or prescribe a precise differentiability class. Its differential interpretation carries smoothness qualifications acknowledged in the discussion of discontinuous solutions [raw PDF page 26, printed page 4, Section 1.1.2]."
}
